//? Title: PixelCNN++: Quantized RGB Logistic Mixtures
//? Subtitle: Whole-pixel causality, exact bin tails and explicit channel-coupled sampling
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A whole-pixel-causal context predicts a mixture of discretized RGB logistic
conditionals. Endpoint bins include infinite tails; green and blue means use
preceding observed channels during likelihood evaluation and preceding sampled,
quantized channels during generation. Worked normalization and coupling checks
precede the original CIFAR-10 teaching protocol: exact integer4x4 area means to
8x8, three width4 gated blocks, three mixture components and48 Adam updates.
The fixed-final test loss is7.48424 bits per8-bit RGB channel, compared with a
fit-only per-channel histogram's7.91458 and uniform8. Matching source records,
causal derivatives and sampled codes establish fidelity of this small run;
they do not establish natural-image quality or published benchmark performance.
)
\toc

> Separate past-pixel context from within-pixel dependence

[Salimans, Karpathy, Chen and Kingma, PixelCNN++: Improving the PixelCNN with
Discretized Logistic Mixture Likelihood and Other Modifications
(2017)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1701.05517) combines whole-pixel conditioning with
a discretized logistic mixture likelihood. This compact teaching variant keeps
that likelihood and channel coupling but uses only three width4 gated blocks.
It has no multiscale downsampling, dropout, labels, pooling, normalization or
batch-coupled statistics. It is not the published large CIFAR-10 model.

The dual-stream context uses vertical3x3 and horizontal1x3 masks. First-block
masks exclude **the whole current RGB pixel**, not just its current channel.
Later vertical current-row features already depend only on earlier raw rows;
horizontal residuals are used after the first block. Each block has an
unbiased1x1 vertical-to-horizontal link, tanh×sigmoid gates and a biased1x1
horizontal projection. A final biased1x1 head emits30 numbers per pixel.
For each of three components the layout is

$$ (a,\mu_R,\mu_G,\mu_B,s_R,s_G,s_B,c_{RG},c_{BR},c_{BG}). $$
\alttext(Each component stores a mixture logit, three means, three log scales, and three raw channel-coupling coefficients.)

All30 numbers depend only on preceding raw pixels. Their mixture logit becomes
a normalized weight through softmax; the other nine parameters define one RGB
conditional. A single component is chosen for the entire RGB pixel, followed
by sequential red, green and blue draws. There is no independent component
selection for each channel.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/pp_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
