> Hand-check all256 bins and their infinite endpoint tails

For one channel, let x=code/127.5−1, s=max(raw_logscale,−7),
h=(x−mu+step)exp(−s), l=(x−mu−step)exp(−s), and
Delta=width exp(−s). The stable interior log mass is

$$ \log p(x)=h-\operatorname{softplus}(h)-\operatorname{softplus}(l)
 +\log[-\operatorname{expm1}(-\Delta)]. $$
\alttext(The stable interior logistic bin log mass uses softplus and expm1 rather than subtracting nearly equal CDF values.)

The source uses Float32 step=1/255 and width=2/255, promoted even in Double
arithmetic. This exact source choice is retained. At the lower endpoint use
−softplus(−h), the mass extending to negative infinity. At the upper endpoint
use−softplus(l), extending to positive infinity. The implemented tail guards
are x<=Float32(−.999) and x>=Float32(.999). For the256 code centers these
select exactly the two endpoint codes. Raw scales clip below−7; ties retain
the raw-scale derivative. No mean clipping or small-mass approximation is used.

A zero-mean unit-scale logistic gives each endpoint a substantial tail mass.
The following no-fit example enumerates every byte, with one component and
zero coupling. The first channel's256 masses sum to one within source rounding;
using finite-width intervals for the endpoint codes would leave mass outside
[-1,1] unaccounted for. All numerical calculations use the native tensor API.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/pp_bin_toy.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
 namespace n=dm::nn;auto t=n::DType::Float64;
 auto codes=n::arange(0,256);auto x=n::cast(codes,t)/127.5-1;
 auto rgb=n::tile(n::reshape(x,{256,1}),{1,3});
 auto out=n::models::DiscretizedLogisticRGB(rgb,n::zeros({256,10},t),1);
 auto logmass=n::reshape(n::slice_axis(out.channel_log_mass,2,0,1),{256});
 auto mass=n::exp(logmass);auto total=n::sum(mass).item();
 if(std::abs(total-1)>2e-5)throw std::runtime_error("Tail normalization");
 const double step=float(1.f/255);
 auto truncated=n::sigmoid(x+step)-n::sigmoid(x-step);
 dm::graphics::RPlot::create().lines(codes.to_vector<double>(),logmass.to_vector<double>(),"Endpoint tails included",{37,99,235})
 .lines(codes.to_vector<double>(),n::log(truncated).to_vector<double>(),"Finite endpoint intervals",{220,38,38})
 .y_limits(-7,2).x_label("8-bit code").y_label("Log bin probability (nats)")
 .title("Infinite endpoint tails restore the omitted probability mass").save_svg("build/pp_bin_toy.svg");
 std::print("Native full-grid mass: {:.8f}; finite-interval-only mass: {:.8f}.\n",total,n::sum(truncated).item());
}
```
