> Check causality at the context head rather than at the coupled likelihood

For each site, differentiate the sum of its30 head parameters with respect to
all64×3 raw input values. All12288 entries are retained; every current/future
RGB derivative is exactly zero. At each of64 sites, negate all current/future
RGB values and require all prefix head parameters to stay exactly unchanged.
These finite-change controls complement derivatives that could be zero only
because a gate locally saturates. Single-image and batched heads agree within
2e−6. Likelihood, derivatives, sampling and completions leave restored weights
bit identical.

The image below reduces **only for display**: maximum absolute derivative over
the three input channels for each64×64 site pair, log-scaled within the figure.
The full three-channel arrays remain in causality.npz. This probes the compact
context head, not an attribution score and not the within-pixel likelihood,
which legitimately depends on current observed R/G through channel coupling.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/pp_causality.png", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
